{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786510218,"nonce":"8bE2AhF0Ksf08QO2MjC7TXVRV2g=","title":"Title: `stats.rollup` has no index for either table it filters: two unbounded scans per org, every 15 minutes","message":"**What is wrong.** `rollupDaySQL` filters two never-reaped tables on `(org_id, \u003ctimestamp\u003e)`, and neither table has an index leading with that pair.\n\nThe `occ` CTE, `internal/stats/repository/rollup.go:75-82`:\n\n```\n    FROM alert_occurrences o\n    JOIN alerts a  ON a.id = o.alert_id AND a.org_id = o.org_id\n   CROSS JOIN bounds b\n   WHERE o.org_id = $1\n     AND NOT a.synthetic\n     AND o.started_at \u003e= b.day_start\n     AND o.started_at \u003c  b.day_end\n```\n\nThe `notif` CTE, `:88-97`:\n\n```\n    FROM notifications n\n   ...\n   WHERE n.org_id = $1\n     AND NOT a.synthetic\n     AND n.created_at \u003e= b.day_start\n     AND n.created_at \u003c  b.day_end\n```\n\n`alert_occurrences` carries five indexes (`db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:187-199`): `(alert_id) WHERE ended_at IS NULL`, `(org_id, alert_id, seq DESC)`, `(org_id, group_id, started_at DESC)`, `(source_ends_at)` partial, and `(org_id, ack_state, started_at DESC) WHERE ended_at IS NULL`. The last is partial on open episodes — precisely the rows this rollup does not count, since it counts `state = 'resolved'` and `state = 'expired'` (`rollup.go:70-71`). None of the five can drive `(org_id, started_at BETWEEN …)`.\n\n`notifications` carries three: `(org_id, subject_kind, subject_id, created_at DESC)` and `(org_id, alert_id, created_at DESC)` (`db/migrations/00011_notification.sql:239,243`), and `(org_id, occurrence_id) WHERE occurrence_id IS NOT NULL` (`db/migrations/00029_delivery_rollup.sql:49`). Every one of them requires a second equality the rollup does not supply; `created_at` is only ever the third or fourth column. There is no `(org_id, created_at)`.\n\n**The demonstration.** `internal/stats/worker/rollup.go:56-72` enumerates every tenant and calls `Rollup` sequentially, one org at a time. `Service.Rollup` runs `RollupDay` `RollupBackfillDays = 2` times per org (`internal/stats/service/rollup.go:26,53-56`). The job is periodic at 15 minutes (`internal/platform/jobs/registry.go:292`) with a 5-minute timeout (`:226`).\n\nSo one tick issues `2 × N` executions of `rollupDaySQL`, each one scanning `alert_occurrences` and `notifications` in full. Both tables are on ADR 0024's never-reaped list (`docs/adr/0024-retention-defaults-and-cold-storage.md:39`), so the scanned volume only ever rises. At 20 orgs that is 40 full passes over both tables inside a 5-minute budget; at 5M occurrences the budget is gone long before the last tenant. The worker logs and continues per tenant (`worker/rollup.go:63-70`), so the visible symptom is not a crash: the hygiene report stops updating for the tenants at the back of the loop, and `oto_jobs_failed_total` climbs on timeout.\n\nThe same missing `alert_occurrences (org_id, started_at)` also drives the `candidates` CTE of `relatedAlertsSQL` (`internal/enrichment/repository/alertread.go:172-181`), which filters on exactly the same three predicates — so one index serves a background job and a request-path enrichment read.\n\n**Why it matters.** Every other list query in oto is deliberate about its driving index; `db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:188-198` documents each occurrence index with the query it serves. The rollup is the one read with no such line, and it is the read whose output an operator is meant to trust enough to delete an alert rule (`internal/stats/service/rollup.go:38-45`). A report that silently stops refreshing is worse than one that is absent.\n\n**Where.** `internal/stats/repository/rollup.go:75-82` and `:88-97` (the two CTEs), `internal/stats/worker/rollup.go:56-72` (the sequential per-org loop), `internal/stats/service/rollup.go:26` (`RollupBackfillDays`), `db/migrations/00007_alerts.sql:187-199` and `db/migrations/00011_notification.sql:239,243` (the index lists that do not cover it), `internal/enrichment/repository/alertread.go:172-181` (the second caller).\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** `alert_occurrences (org_id, started_at)` and `notifications (org_id, created_at)` exist, each with the `COMMENT ON INDEX` naming the query it serves as the neighbouring indexes do, and both rollup CTEs plan as index ranges rather than sequential scans.","files":null}]}